questions.question:
filter vs reduce (for filtering): Remove odds from [1,2,3,4,5] using both. Which is clearer and why?
// with filter
// with reduce

questions.answer:
Using filter is more clear and expressive for removing odds, because it directly describes the intent of filtering. Reduce can achieve the same result but is less readable in this context.

const numbers = [1,2,3,4,5];

// with filter
const evenNumbers = numbers.filter(num => num % 2 === 0);
console.log(evenNumbers); // [2,4]

// with reduce
const evenNumbers2 = numbers.reduce((acc, currentVal) => {
  if (currentVal % 2 === 0) {
    acc.push(currentVal);
  }
  return acc;
}, []);
console.log(evenNumbers2); // [2,4]

filter always returns an array with all matching elements while find returns the first matching element or undefined. Find short-circuits and stops once it finds a match, but filter checks
the entire array. You use findIndex when you need the position of the first match, for example if you want to update or remove that element.

const nums = [10,20,30];

// filter
console.log(nums.filter(n => n > 15)); // [20,30]

// find
console.log(nums.find(n => n > 15)); // 20

// findIndex
const idx = nums.findIndex(n => n > 15);
nums[idx] = 99;
console.log(nums); // [10,99,30]

some returns true if at least one element passes the test, while every requires all elements to pass.
Both methods short-circuit: some stops once it finds the first true result, and every stops once it finds the first false result.

const nums = [1, 2, 3];

// some → stops early when x > 2
console.log(nums.some(x => x > 2)); // true

// every → stops early when x < 2 fails
console.log(nums.every(x => x > 2)); // false

// real example
const users = [
  { name: 'Dan', isOnline: true },
  { name: 'Lea', isOnline: false }
];

console.log(users.some(u => u.isOnline)); // true (at least one online)
console.log(users.every(u => u.isOnline)); // false (not all online)

questions.question:
includes vs indexOf: How do they differ in handling NaN? Provide a tiny demo.
const xs = [1, NaN, 3];
// includes?
// indexOf?

questions.answer:
includes checks if a value exists in the array and returns true or false, while indexOf returns the index of the value or -1 if not found.
The key difference is that includes uses SameValueZero comparison, so it correctly detects NaN, while indexOf uses === and fails to find NaN.

const xs = [1, NaN, 3];

// includes can detect NaN
console.log(xs.includes(NaN)); // true

// indexOf cannot detect NaN
console.log(xs.indexOf(NaN)); // -1

questions.question:
slice vs splice: Remove the 3rd element from an array in two ways and explain which method mutates.
const a = [1,2,3,4];
// non-mutating remove with slice
// mutating remove with splice

questions.answer:
slice does not mutate the original array, while splice does.
You can remove the 3rd element using slice by combining two parts into a new array, and with splice by directly deleting it from the original.

const a = [1, 2, 3, 4];

// non-mutating remove with slice
const sliced1 = a.slice(0, 2);
const sliced2 = a.slice(3, 4);
const newSliced = sliced1.concat(sliced2);
console.log(newSliced); // [1, 2, 4]
console.log(a); // [1, 2, 3, 4]

// mutating remove with splice
const removed = a.splice(2, 1);
console.log(removed); // [3]
console.log(a); // [1, 2, 4]

questions.question:
sort pitfalls: Why does ['10','2','1'].sort() produce a surprising order? Provide two correct numeric comparators (asc/desc).
// asc
// desc

questions.answer:
By default, sort() converts elements to strings and compares them lexicographically (like words), not numerically. So ['10','2','1'].sort() returns ['1','10','2'] because '1' comes before '2' when compared as text.
To sort numbers correctly, provide a numeric comparator.

const nums = ['10','2','1'];

// ascending numeric order
console.log(nums.sort((a, b) => a - b)); // ['1','2','10']

// descending numeric order
console.log(nums.sort((a, b) => b - a)); // ['10','2','1']

questions.question:
localeCompare for strings: Sort ['é','e','ê'] correctly for French and then reverse it.
// ascending (fr)
// descending (fr)

questions.answer:
localeCompare compares strings according to language rules instead of Unicode code points. By providing a locale (like 'fr' for French), you can correctly sort accented letters.
Without localeCompare, ['é','e','ê'] would not appear in proper French order.

const letters = ['é','e','ê'];

// ascending (French)
const asc = letters.slice().sort((a, b) => a.localeCompare(b, 'fr'));
console.log(asc); // ['e','é','ê']

// descending (French)
const desc = letters.slice().sort((a, b) => b.localeCompare(a, 'fr'));
console.log(desc); // ['ê','é','e']

push adds an element to the end of an array and pop removes from the end. unshift adds to the beginning and shift removes from the beginning.
Performance-wise, push and pop are fast because they only affect the last element, while shift and unshift are slow for large arrays since they must reindex all elements.

const arr = [1, 2, 3];

// push / pop (end)
arr.push(4); // [1,2,3,4]
arr.pop(); // [1,2,3]

// unshift / shift (start)
arr.unshift(0); // [0,1,2,3]
arr.shift(); // [1,2,3]

To get the total price of in-stock items over $50, first filter the array to keep only items where inStock is true and price > 50, then reduce to sum their prices. map is optional if you prefer to extract prices before reducing.

const orders = [
  { price: 40, inStock: true },
  { price: 75, inStock: false },
  { price: 100, inStock: true }
];

const total = orders
  .filter(order => order.inStock && order.price > 50)
  .reduce((sum, order) => sum + order.price, 0);

console.log(total); // 100

Modern JavaScript engines implement a stable Array.prototype.sort, meaning elements with equal keys keep their original order. In older engines, stability was not guaranteed. To ensure stable behavior manually, you can include each element's original index and compare it when keys are equal.

const students = [
  { name: 'Tom', grade: 90 },
  { name: 'Ana', grade: 80 },
  { name: 'Ben', grade: 90 },
  { name: 'Liz', grade: 70 }
];

const withIndex = students.map((s, i) => ({ ...s, index: i }));
const sorted = withIndex.sort((a, b) => {
  if (a.grade !== b.grade) return b.grade - a.grade;
  return a.index - b.index; // preserve original order for equal grades
});

console.log(sorted);

When appending many small arrays into one large array, concat, push(...), and preallocation differ in speed and memory use.

1. big = big.concat(small): Creates a new array every time, copying all elements. Slower for large loops but keeps immutability.
2. big.push(...small): Mutates the existing array and is faster since no copying occurs. Less safe if you need the original array unchanged.
3. Preallocate + index writes: Fastest method if the final size is known; you can preallocate an array and fill it by index in a loop, but it's more complex to write.

Example:
let big = [];
const small = [1,2,3];

// concat (slow, creates new array)
for(let i=0;i<1000;i++) big = big.concat(small);

// push (faster, mutates array)
for(let i=0;i<1000;i++) big.push(...small);

// preallocate (fastest if size known)
const total = 1000 * small.length;
const big2 = new Array(total);
let idx = 0;
for(let i=0;i<1000;i++) {
  for(let j=0;j<small.length;j++) {
    big2[idx++] = small[j];
  }
}

flatMap maps each element and flattens one level of arrays. When some objects may have missing or empty properties, you can safely return an empty array to avoid errors. Using u.addresses || [] ensures flatMap always receives an array.

